<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matsudaira Sadaaki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matsudaira Sadaaki was the last lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a> domain. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> deputy from 1864 he worked alongside his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-katamori">Matsudaira Katamori</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> to defend the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s position in the capital. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> he fought for the shogunate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a>, surrendering in 1869, and was later pardoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matsudaira-sadaaki.jpg" alt="Matsudaira Sadaaki" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 不明。 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matudaira_Sadaaki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Matsudaira Tetsunosuke on 18 January 1847 at Ichigaya in Edo, he was the eighth son of Matsudaira Yoshitatsu, daimyō of the Takasu Domain; his elder brothers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-katamori">Matsudaira Katamori</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshikatsu">Tokugawa Yoshikatsu</a> and Tokugawa Mochinaga of the Owari Domain. In 1859 he was adopted posthumously as heir to Matsudaira Sadamichi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a>, of the Hisamatsu Matsudaira clan descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>'s brother Hisamatsu Sadakatsu, and was betrothed to Sadamichi's three-year-old daughter Matsudaira Hatsu. In 1863 he accompanied Shogun Tokugawa Iemochi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, the first shogunal visit there in 230 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He served as Kyoto Shoshidai from 16 May 1864 to 3 January 1868, the last holder of the post, which oversaw relations with the imperial court and the administration of Kyoto and eight provinces. The appointment was unusual, as the office normally went to an older fudai daimyō. He supported his brother Katamori, the Kyoto Shugoshoku, deployed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a> troops in the Kinmon incident and against the Tengu-tō uprising, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a> held the authority of a rōjū. He was known as a keen horseman who rode the streets in western military dress.</p><p>After Yoshinobu resigned in November 1867, Sadaaki withdrew with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>. Following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-toba-fushimi">Battle of Toba–Fushimi</a> he sailed to Kashiwazaki in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echigo-province">Echigo Province</a>, an exclave of his domain, then fought on at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a>, went to the Yonezawa Domain for reinforcements, and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enomoto-takeaki">Enomoto Takeaki</a>'s fleet to the Ezo Republic. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakodate">Hakodate</a> fell he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, but lacking funds he returned to Yokohama and surrendered. Kuwana Castle had already yielded without resistance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pardoned and released in 1872, he married Hatsu, studied English at a school founded by the missionary Samuel Robbins Brown, and in 1874 accompanied his son Matsudaira Sadanori to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rutgers-university">Rutgers University</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a> of 1877 he led former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a> samurai for the Imperial Japanese Army. From 1894 he was one of the kannushi of the Nikkō Tōshō-gū with Katamori and Saigō Tanomo. The diarist Clara Whitney recorded that he was a skilled organ player. He died on 12 July 1908 and is buried in the Somei Cemetery in Tokyo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matsudaira Sadaaki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its last lord, <b>Matsudaira Sadaaki</b>, served as Kyoto deputy alongside his brother Matsudaira Katamori of Aizu.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuwana-domain">Kuwana Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1886" aria-label="Edo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Matsudaira Sadaaki"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4293 4483h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4293 4483h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1886">What was here in 1886? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
